WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.629
In the 30 years since it hit theaters, virtually
every superhero movie has been influenced

00:00:04.629 --> 00:00:07.180
by what was first accomplished in Batman '89.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:11.700
Its impact has been so massive that in a world
where it set the standards, you might not

00:00:11.719 --> 00:00:14.009
have even noticed how much of an effect it
had.

00:00:15.009 --> 00:00:18.560
For those of you who don't remember what it
was like back in the days leading up to June

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.740
23, 1989, it cannot be stressed enough that
Batman was everywhere that summer.

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:28.880
Even in a year crowded with massively successful
franchise movies like Indiana Jones and the

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:34.050
Last Crusade Ghostbusters II, Back to the
Future II and the beginning of the "Disney

00:00:34.050 --> 00:00:38.580
Renaissance" with The Little Mermaid, the
Dark Knight was pop culture's most inescapable

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:39.080
character.

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.820
Of course, movie marketing had existed for
as long as movies had, but the Batman marketing

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:45.440
was on a whole different level.

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:50.480
There were countless tie-ins, from toys to
breakfast cereals to a concept album by Prince,

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.220
all of which arrived with some of the strongest
branding ever.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.940
Rather than putting the spotlight on director
Tim Burton, stars Michael Keaton and Jack

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:03.400
Nicholson, or even the character of Batman
himself, the marketing was overwhelmingly

00:01:03.400 --> 00:01:06.060
focused on the iconic image of Batman's emblem.

00:01:06.470 --> 00:01:10.740
It started with the first poster, which was
just the emblem and a release date, and continued

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:14.960
across countless t-shirts, hats, action figures,
and pretty much everything else in the meantime.

00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:19.859
Even the opening sequence of the movie itself
was just an extreme close-up of that logo

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:21.160
underneath the credits.

00:01:21.509 --> 00:01:26.260
By reducing the entire idea of Batman down
to that symbol, using the branding that had

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:31.249
already been refined over the past 50 years
of comics and pop culture, Warner Bros. seared

00:01:31.249 --> 00:01:37.200
that image into moviegoers' minds and sold
an estimated $500 million in merchandise alone.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:43.960
Adjusting for inflation, that's over $1 billion
today, all before we even get to the box office

00:01:43.960 --> 00:01:45.159
take for the movie itself.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:49.780
"Hubba hubba hubba, money money money, who
do ya trust?

00:01:49.789 --> 00:01:50.789
Me?

00:01:50.789 --> 00:01:52.100
I'm givin' away free money!"

00:01:53.020 --> 00:01:57.420
For a movie about a character famously known
for being super-competent at crime-fighting

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:02.290
and having a plan for every eventuality, one
of the weirdest moments of Batman '89 comes

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:03.420
right at the beginning.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:08.660
The very first thing we see Batman do after
he glides silently onto a rooftop to confront

00:02:08.660 --> 00:02:12.840
two criminals who have just committed a mugging
is get shot and fall down.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.660
Seriously: this is our introduction to the
Dark Knight, finally returning to the big

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:19.200
screen for the first time in 23 years.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:24.180
To be fair, the crucial element here is that
after being shot, Batman gets back up.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:29.560
It also showcases one of the movie's changes
to Batman: his costume isn't just a costume,

00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.600
it's bulletproof body armor.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:34.340
"Wait a minute what is that?”

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.040
“Some kinda body armor."

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:38.160
"He's human after all!"

00:02:38.440 --> 00:02:43.580
Before the movie, Batman's costume had usually
been depicted as being, well, a costume, meant

00:02:43.580 --> 00:02:46.500
to frighten criminals more than to protect
the person wearing it.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:51.180
The filmmakers apparently assumed that an
audience could accept rocket cars and killer

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:54.650
clowns, but believing that someone could fight
crime without ever taking a bullet to the

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:56.740
chest was a little too tough to believe.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:03:02.500
That sparked a trend in superhero movies towards
going to great lengths to give a functional

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:05.800
justification for why the superheroes were
wearing these costumes.

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:10.540
It's easy to draw a direct line from Batman's
body armor to the way the X-Men talked about

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.660
their leather suits in the year 2000…

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:15.160
"You actually go outside in these things?"

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:17.900
"What would you prefer, yellow spandex?"

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:23.920
.... and all the way through the conversation
in 2017's Justice League about how the Flash's

00:03:23.920 --> 00:03:26.760
costume was made from space shuttle parts.

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:32.620
If there's one thing superhero movies love,
it's an origin story, and Batman '89 is no

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:33.120
exception.

00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:35.400
In fact, it doesn't just stop with one origin.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:36.680
It gives us two.

00:03:36.940 --> 00:03:41.340
Not only are we treated to the first of many
times that we'll see the Wayne Family's ill-fated

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:46.770
trip down Crime Alley on the big screen, but
most of the plot revolves around another fateful

00:03:46.770 --> 00:03:50.980
story about who a character is and how they
came to be: the Joker.

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:52.940
"Terrorizes?

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:59.180
Wait'll they get a load of me."

00:03:59.460 --> 00:04:03.420
Unlike the comics, where his identity has
always been shrouded in mystery to the point

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:08.430
of having conflicting origins, the movie gave
us the straightforward story of Jack Napier,

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:12.920
whose nearly unforgivable pun name and lucky
deck of cards lead him to take the identity

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:13.760
of the Joker.

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:18.539
It seems like an odd choice, if only because
there's no real change to the character after

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:20.320
he takes his ill-fated acid bath.

00:04:20.669 --> 00:04:25.460
He's already a killer with a thing for playing
cards and a wardrobe full of purple suits;

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:29.100
his emergence as the Joker just makes his
crimes a little more artistic.

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.940
"Gentlemen… let's broaden our minds."

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:40.200
Batman '89's most crucial plot change was
making Jack Napier the crook who shot Batman's

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:40.700
parents.

00:04:40.820 --> 00:04:44.960
That's never been a part of the comics, where
the Waynes were shot by a mugger named Joe

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:48.140
Chill, as we would eventually see in Batman
Begins.

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:52.999
The intent here was to give Batman a personal
connection to his arch-enemy that would go

00:04:52.999 --> 00:04:57.889
beyond just criminal vs. crimefighter, which
brought the superheroic story more in line

00:04:57.889 --> 00:04:59.420
with a traditional action movie.

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:01.860
"You killed my parents

00:05:02.260 --> 00:05:02.760
What?

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:05.080
Haha.

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:06.379
What are you talking about?"

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:09.860
"I made you, you made me first."

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:15.140
That set the tone for virtually every superhero
movie that came after, with personal connections

00:05:15.140 --> 00:05:18.980
built into heroes and villains, even when
they never existed in the comics.

00:05:19.570 --> 00:05:24.020
In Iron Man, for instance, Obadiah Stane wasn't
just the rival businessman that readers knew

00:05:24.020 --> 00:05:27.940
from the comics , he was a father figure for
Tony Stark who betrayed him.

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:32.900
Along the same lines, the Erik Killmonger
seen in the Black Panther movie has a familial

00:05:32.900 --> 00:05:36.200
tie to T'Challa that his comic book counterpart
never did.

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:38.060
"Hey Auntie."

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:40.740
That's not limited to superheroes, either.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:42.800
This idea also had an effect on the Joker.

00:05:43.169 --> 00:05:46.900
While Heath Ledger's performance in The Dark
Knight retained the character's mysterious,

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:52.180
often conflicting origins, the upcoming Joker
solo film is doing the exact opposite.

00:05:52.660 --> 00:05:57.509
It's even set to include both Thomas and Bruce
Wayne as characters, giving Joaquin Phoenix's

00:05:57.509 --> 00:06:01.520
Joker the same sort of personal connection
to Batman that we saw in 1989.

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:07.139
In the comics, Batman has traditionally been
depicted as a character who doesn't kill his

00:06:07.139 --> 00:06:11.330
enemies, if only because it's pretty tough
for the Riddler to show up again for his next

00:06:11.330 --> 00:06:13.520
story if Batman kicks his head off in this
one.

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:18.500
In the movie, however, he racks up a body
count that doesn't just rival the Joker's,

00:06:18.500 --> 00:06:19.840
it goes well beyond it.

00:06:19.980 --> 00:06:25.050
He shoots at his opponents with vehicle-mounted
machine guns, blows up a factory full of goons

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:29.910
with Batmobile bombs, tosses a dude to his
death from a bell tower, and, in the end,

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:34.280
has a pretty direct hand in killing the Joker
as revenge for the Joker murdering his parents.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:39.180
That idea, of treating superhero stories like
action movies, where the ending always comes

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:42.540
with the death of the bad guy, didn't necessarily
start with Batman.

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.160
Nine years earlier, Superman II ended with
the Man of Steel tossing the Phantom Zone

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:49.880
Criminals down a bottomless pit in the Fortress
of Solitude.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:55.860
Still, Batman codified it, setting up a pattern
of supervillains dying at the end of movies,

00:06:55.860 --> 00:06:59.620
often at the hands of the good guys, that
wouldn't really be subverted until Spider-Man:

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:03.920
Homecoming, where the villain whose life gets
spared at the end is played by...

00:07:04.180 --> 00:07:05.060
Michael Keaton.

00:07:06.280 --> 00:07:10.880
When you think of Gotham City, the image that
comes to mind is a sprawling, smoke-filled

00:07:10.880 --> 00:07:11.780
urban nightmare.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:16.800
It's a place with deep shadows and gargoyles
leering down from every building, where clean

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:21.210
Art Deco designs are shoved right up against
buildings that might as well be modern gothic

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:21.880
castles.

00:07:22.250 --> 00:07:26.360
It's a city that seems built for a character
like Batman, and that's an idea that pretty

00:07:26.380 --> 00:07:27.960
much starts with this movie.

00:07:28.580 --> 00:07:32.680
Gotham City has, of course, always had its
share of character, but it was mostly meant

00:07:32.680 --> 00:07:34.300
to be relatively realistic.

00:07:34.860 --> 00:07:38.259
As longtime Batman writer and editor Denny
O'Neil put it, Gotham was

00:07:38.500 --> 00:07:43.140
"Manhattan below Fourteenth Street at eleven
minutes past midnight on the coldest night

00:07:43.140 --> 00:07:43.880
in November."

00:07:44.660 --> 00:07:46.560
All of that changed with the film.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:51.120
Tim Burton's greatest strength has always
been in his distinctive visuals, and he and

00:07:51.120 --> 00:07:55.340
production designer Anton Furst put that on
display in Batman '89.

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:59.720
They recreated Gotham as an environment that
was less like the New York you could actually

00:07:59.729 --> 00:08:05.689
visit and more like, as Burton memorably described
it, a city where "hell burst through the pavement

00:08:05.689 --> 00:08:06.360
and grew."

00:08:06.680 --> 00:08:11.599
The aesthetic stuck, too Furst's designs were
quickly incorporated into the comics canon

00:08:11.600 --> 00:08:17.140
in 1990, and Gotham has retained that distinctly
hellish character ever since.

00:08:17.540 --> 00:08:23.159
In 1978, Superman the Movie brought the Man
of Steel to our world; in 1989, Burton and

00:08:23.159 --> 00:08:25.449
Furst took us to Batman's city instead.

00:08:26.740 --> 00:08:31.699
In a lot of ways, Batman '89 was far more
inspired by the '60s TV show than the comics

00:08:31.700 --> 00:08:37.100
of the '80s, with its brightly colored knockout
gas, dutch angles, and over-the-top villains.

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:41.900
These are all elements that hearken back to
Adam West's tenure as the Caped Crusader,

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:43.620
which makes a lot of sense.

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:49.380
As much as the late '80s brought in groundbreaking
comics like The Dark Knight Returns and Batman:

00:08:49.820 --> 00:08:53.360
Year One, decades of syndication had cemented
the show as the most prominent version of

00:08:53.360 --> 00:08:55.180
the character in the larger pop culture.

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:59.380
Burton did, however, draw influence from further
back in Batman's history.

00:08:59.760 --> 00:09:03.840
In an effort to evoke the character's roots,
the style of the movie is largely inspired

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:06.640
by the fashions and aesthetics of the 1940s.

00:09:07.100 --> 00:09:12.370
It's clearly set in the modern day there are
computers, modern cars, and, you know, Prince

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:18.040
but characters like Jack Napier and Alex Knox
wear distinctly '40s-style suits, complete

00:09:18.040 --> 00:09:18.700
with hats.

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:23.540
Even Harvey Dent's campaign for District Attorney
is a visual reference to Citizen Kane.

00:09:24.040 --> 00:09:28.810
The overall effect is that the movie's visuals
are a very interesting blend of Batman's past

00:09:28.810 --> 00:09:34.279
and present, but in retrospect, it also serves
as a sort of proof-of-concept for the superhero

00:09:34.280 --> 00:09:35.529
movie as a period piece.

00:09:35.720 --> 00:09:39.600
It would take years for this to really pay
off, but it's hard to imagine a world where

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:44.440
we got to movies like Captain America: The
First Avenger or X-Men: First Class without

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:47.540
Batman paving the way for superheroes with
a retro aesthetic.

00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:52.699
Imagine that you're the head of a movie studio
in July of 1989.

00:09:52.699 --> 00:09:57.259
Batman is everywhere, and you've watched while
it took ten days to become the fastest movie

00:09:57.260 --> 00:10:00.220
to ever rack up $100 million at the box office.

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:03.060
You want another piece of that massive success.

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:07.710
Obviously, the best thing to do here is to
make more superhero movies and capitalize

00:10:07.710 --> 00:10:10.500
on the characters that the public already
loves, right?

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:13.980
"You… are my number one guy."

00:10:15.420 --> 00:10:15.980
Wrong.

00:10:16.540 --> 00:10:21.400
If you're making movies in 1989, you apparently
look at the two highest grossing movies of

00:10:21.400 --> 00:10:26.370
the year, Indiana Jones and the Last Crusade
and Batman, and decide that moviegoers are

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:30.000
just into movies about stuff that was popular
in the '30s and '40s.

00:10:30.430 --> 00:10:34.640
The years after Batman's titanic success saw
a sudden flood of movies that attempted to

00:10:34.640 --> 00:10:37.660
capitalize on what studios clearly hoped was
a trend.

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:45.060
Dick Tracy in 1990, The Shadow in 1994, and
The Phantom in 1996 were all revivals of characters

00:10:45.060 --> 00:10:50.200
who had been hugely popular five decades earlier
that leaned into their pulpy aesthetics.

00:10:50.470 --> 00:10:56.400
The Rocketeer in 1991 and The Mask in 1994
were both based on newer comics that had been

00:10:56.410 --> 00:11:00.540
created in the '80s, but that were brought
to the screen with the same kind of retro

00:11:00.540 --> 00:11:01.500
'40s visuals.

00:11:01.860 --> 00:11:07.319
It even extended to TV, where Darkwing Duck
secret identity: Drake Mallard hit airwaves

00:11:07.319 --> 00:11:13.529
in 1991 as a full-on parody of The Shadow
secret identity: Kent Allard a character with

00:11:13.529 --> 00:11:16.240
whom its target audience had zero familiarity.

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:21.600
Unfortunately for the studio executives who
really wanted audiences to love the Phantom

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:25.680
like they did when they were eight, this was
a false-start of a trend to jump on.

00:11:26.500 --> 00:11:31.450
While there were some good movies in that
throwback crop, none were as memorable, beloved,

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:33.800
or even remotely as successful as Batman.

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:38.060
It turned out that the public was far less
interested in characters who hit their pop

00:11:38.060 --> 00:11:42.791
cultural peak 50 years ago than they were
in actual superhero stories, and it would

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:46.160
take another decade or two for movie studios
to come around to that one.

